messages:
incident-bot: ALERT: spike in 429s and request queue growth on /v1/generate (us-west-2). Observed 12k 429s in last 5m, error rate 18%. Triggering pager.
Maya: I'm on pager. seeing large fanout from a single API key. looks like bursty embedding job + streaming clients.
Ryan: grabbing logs now. Can someone confirm if Dedicated pools are healthy? pinging eng-runtime.
Sanjay: runtime metrics: dedicated pool us-west-gpu-a is at 92% GPU mem, 88% NVLink; queue length up but no OOMs. We have degraded throughput due to long-seq requests.
Eva: CS reports 3 customers flagged, one enterprise reported degraded UX on chat feature. ETA on mitigations?
Tom: recommend immediate soft per-key token quota enforcement and edge shaping. We can toggle temporary token-level max_concurrency to 6 and reduce burst window.
Maya: agree. also propose enabling automatic model-fallback to smaller variant for long sequences to reduce latency.
Ryan: I've created a mitigation runbook entry and will be the mitigation lead. Patch incoming: /ops/edge/shaper -> set per_token_max=6, burst=30s -> 15s. Applying now.
ops-bot: Command accepted: set_shaper --scope=token --region=us-west-2 --max_concurrency=6 --burst_seconds=15 by Ryan
incident-bot: ACTION: edge shaper updated. Expect reduced 429s in 2-3min. Notifying SDK/devex to publish guidance.
Maya: SDK/DevEx note: we need to tell clients to backoff more conservatively and use idempotency headers for retries. Preparing guidance blurb.
Sanjay: Runtime suggestion: enable transient routing to smaller quantized model variant for requests with seq_len > 2048. Will enable canary on 10%.
Eva: CS draft to customers: "We're currently rate-limiting bursty traffic to protect overall availability. If you see 429s, please implement exponential backoff with jitter and include an idempotency key to safely retry." Want approval before sending to enterprise contacts.
Tom: approve. add SDK examples.
Maya: SDK guidance (quick):

JS example:
```
const MAX_RETRIES = 6;
async function generateWithRetry(req) {
  let retries = 0;
  let backoff = 500; // ms
  while (retries < MAX_RETRIES) {
    try {
      return await client.generate(req, {
        headers: { 'Idempotency-Key': req.idempotencyKey }
      });
    } catch (e) {
      if (e.status === 429 || e.status === 503) {
        await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, backoff + Math.floor(Math.random()*backoff)));
        backoff = Math.min(backoff * 2, 8000);
        retries++;
        continue;
      }
      throw e;
    }
  }
  throw new Error('max retries exceeded');
}
```

Maya: Notes: - use exponential backoff starting 500ms, factor 2, capped at 8s, add full jitter. - prefer idempotency header X-Idempotency-Key or Idempotency-Key depending on SDK. - for streaming clients: reduce parallel streams, batch tokens locally then send; if using chunked streaming, pause/resume on 429s.
Ryan: Ops update: edge shaper roll-out complete. 429s dropped to 6k after 3m, now 2.1k at 10m. Queue length halved. Continuing to monitor.
Sanjay: Canaries enabled for fallback model; initial perf looks fine, latency down ~18% for long seq. Will scale canary if stable.
Eva: Sent approved note to affected customers, flagged enterprise contact for handshake.
Maya: Can we add a short-term SDK patch that exposes a built-in retry helper with idempotency header and jittered backoff? That would reduce customer churn.
Tom: +1. Ryan, can you add follow-up task for devex to ship patch and blog blurb once incident settled?
Ryan: noted and creating ticket: DEVEX-4521. Owner: Maya. ETA 48h.
incident-bot: INCIDENT UPDATE: mitigation in place, partial customer impact, no data loss. Oncall: Ryan -> handing off to Tom for night watch.
Tom: Taking watch. will escalate if 429 rate stops improving or if any enterprise reports persistent failures. Leaving monitoring alerts pinned.
Maya: Leaving a short postmortem stub in incidents channel. Will collect prompts and affected endpoints for rollout.
ops-bot: Reminder: revert shaper changes after traffic normalizes or after 4h. Use command: revert_shaper --change=2410009876
Ryan: Thanks all. good teamwork. :thumbsup:
